Every pre-existing row's colour on every leg is CARRIED from tables 12 and 13a (the same source bytes, the same
sessions: repair14_20260916/logs/, table13a_20260925/logs/).  What is PREDICTED here is the 14 new rows, per leg, from
each mutant's hunk:
  tl <op>       CHANNEL: s1's magnitude.  KILLS M3_timelatch (TIME exempted once the latch is SET: the +0x07 refusal
                sets it, the TIME refusal falls into `crime_update_crime_time(d)' (:393), which adds 66 per microsecond
                since the tick that followed s0 -- ~3 s of debugger commands, ~2e8 >> SMALL 6.6e6), M4_timeplain
                (TIME exempted in either latch state) and M1_gotolatch (every refusal after the first falls through).
                GREEN on every other leg: no other hunk reaches a narrow access at 0x38 -- G_glm_tim4clear's is
                `len == 4' at TIME with the latch CLEAR, and the tl sessions read TIME at 8 bytes.
  tl <op> ctl   CONTROL: large on every leg (s2 follows s1's own update, leak or not).
  rl <op> pc    CHANNEL: pc after the refused access.  KILLS M5_revlatch (REV exempted once the latch is SET) and
                M1_gotolatch: the access falls into the REV arm, whose PROM-window test (:344) is TRUE at 0xbfc05180
                and does not test writeflag, so all four ops set pc = ra = 0x80004000.  GREEN on every other leg.
  ctldog <w> msg CHANNEL: the CONTROL text count.  KILLS D_nodogclear (1 -> 2).  GREEN on every other leg: the session
                holds no refusal (no decline-suppression mutant is reached), no HARD_RESET (the K_ legs), and its latch
                is CLEAR (W_ctl8w and G_lane_ctl key on the latch SET).

THE D7 x Q3 INTERLOCK, tested here rather than assumed: M3 and M5 are predicted to red ONLY latch-SET rows (tl, rl) and
NO latch-CLEAR row (time, rev) -- the ruling's "the open cells are {observable label} x {latch SET}" -- while M4, the
plain label leak, reds both.

CHECKS THE PRE-TABLE BLOCK OWES:
  SIBLING PARITY  The five observable labels after repair 15, latch SET: INTSTAT istat sb/lh/sh and INTMASK imask
                  sb/lh/sh (each session's `lb' at the label is its FIRST refusal); CONTROL sb08's `sb 0xff', sh08b's
                  three later `sh', gate run's `sh 0x0800' (a narrow READ at CONTROL is EQUIVALENT: the arm acts only
                  on writes); TIME tl, all four ops; REV rl, all four.  ASYMMETRY: at INTSTAT, INTMASK and CONTROL no
                  row refuses `lb' with the latch SET, while TIME and REV now carry all four ops.  DISPOSITION: the
                  decline block tests neither len nor writeflag, so a width-keyed exemption is a two-axis joint outside
                  the floor -- clause (e); the four-op form at TIME/REV is repair 14's ruling C, a strengthening.
                  Not owed.
  CLAIM -> ROW    Sentences repair 15 adds that assert an observable: "crime_update_crime_time() at the refused access
                  moves the next sample" -> tl; "the extra +0x07 access adds debugger steps, never a TIME update, on a
                  fix build" -> tl on base; "the arm's PROM-window hack redirects the pc to ra" -> rl; "D_nodogclear
                  ... prints for 0x0200" -> ctldog; "`mid nodflt' already reads it after a latch-setting refusal:
                  M1_gotolatch reddened it in table 12" -> repair14_20260916/logs/probe_M1_gotolatch.raw, whose FAILING
                  line carries it, and M1's set above.  NO-ROW sentences: DOG is a bare `break;' (:388-390); a silent
                  decline of the DOG-only write is EQUIVALENT; the latch x DOG joint is clause (e).
  GUARD vs BODY   M3/M5's exemption reads d->warned (CRIME_W_LEN) and relative_addr; the TIME body reads
                  d->last_microseconds and d->reg[TIME], the REV body cpu->pc and gpr[RA].  `grep -n warned
                  src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c': ONE writer (:315, `d->warned |= CRIME_W_LEN', in the decline block) --
                  no other code can set the bit, and the carriers build the SET state directly (the +0x07 refusal).
                  D_nodogclear: guard and body are one lvalue (idata).
  CHANNEL         Per new row, above.  AND THE CHECK THE MANDATE ASKS OF A NEW RULE'S EARLY APPLICATIONS: each new
                  row's channel was named at writing time together with the mutant it kills; check14.py scores whether
                  each named channel moved under its named mutant and stayed put everywhere else.
  ROW-LIST DIFF   (the 2026-08-28 rule) Repair 14's ruling C prescribed the TIME and REV carriers at all four ops; all
                  eight are here.  My design note (scratchpad p529/repair15_design.md, 2026-09-25 ~23:30) listed
                  `tl lb/sh' and `rl lb/sb' only -- that was the note's error against the ruling's own words, and this
                  list is its superset.  Nothing a prior pre-table block prescribed is dropped.

